+## Command guard and the careful-full-access mode
+
+`dsh` ships a deletion guard so misparsed destructive commands cannot take your files with them. The host-plane `command-guard` plugin (`packages/guard/command-guard`) listens on every `pwsh`/`bash` call before dispatch: a zero-cost in-process lexical pre-scan (destructive verb/alias table, cmd-style switches, `.NET` deletion calls, dynamic markers) gates a PowerShell AST pass, and classifies the call into four tiers — **disaster** (denied in EVERY mode: drive roots, root wildcards, UNC/extended roots, the user profile, system directories, the workspace root, `Format-*`/`Clear-Disk`/`diskpart clean`, `robocopy /MIR` into a protected root, recursive `.NET` deletion), **high-risk** (asked through the ordinary approval channel), **normal** (allowed), and **unparseable** (fail-closed; `iex` never slips through). Every non-allow judgment is audited as a log-only `command-guard/decision` session event, and a prompt section teaches the deletion discipline.
+
+The new `careful-full-access` sandbox mode keeps the full-access experience and adds deletion caution: every non-disaster deletion first runs a WhatIf dry run (`$WhatIfPreference = $true`, so PowerShell itself resolves the REAL scope), plus a subtree enumeration for recursive directory targets, then the model-check two-step protocol — the first submission is denied with a bounded preview summary ("this deletion resolves to N objects … re-send the identical command to confirm execution"), and an identical resubmission (session-scoped command fingerprint, TTL'd, one-shot) executes without re-previewing; unpreviewable deletions are denied outright. The mode sits as the middle rung of the permission presets (`workspace-write` → `careful-full-access` → `danger-full-access`), persisted through the ordinary settings mechanism.
+
+Under the hood, the `sandbox-windows-acl` backend additionally splits each workspace grant into two ACEs — an inherit-only full-Modify ACE for descendants and a root-only ACE without DELETE/FILE_DELETE_CHILD — so the confined child can delete/rename/git inside the tree but can never delete or rename the workspace root object itself.
+
 ## Community and support
 
 - Feel free to submit feedback or bug reports through [GitHub Discussions](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions).
